---
components: ["TaskStatusList"]
description: Task status lists show the progress of a multi-step operation, such as provisioning a cluster, with each step's status and time and an overall summary.
---

<script>
  import { TaskStatusList } from "carbon-components-svelte";

  /** @typedef {import("carbon-components-svelte/TaskStatusList/TaskStatusList.svelte").TaskStep} TaskStep */

  const started = Date.now() - 82000;

  /** @type {TaskStep[]} */
  const steps = [
    { id: "vpc", label: "Create VPC", status: "succeeded", startedAt: started, endedAt: started + 12000 },
    { id: "subnets", label: "Create subnets", status: "succeeded", startedAt: started + 12000, endedAt: started + 41000 },
    { id: "cluster", label: "Create cluster", status: "running", startedAt: started + 41000, detail: "2 of 3 nodes ready" },
    { id: "ingress", label: "Install ingress", status: "pending" },
  ];

  /** @type {TaskStep[]} */
  const statuses = [
    { id: "a", label: "Create VPC", status: "succeeded", startedAt: 0, endedAt: 12000 },
    { id: "b", label: "Create cluster", status: "running", startedAt: started },
    { id: "c", label: "Install ingress", status: "pending" },
    { id: "d", label: "Configure network", status: "failed", startedAt: 0, endedAt: 31000 },
    { id: "e", label: "Seed sample data", status: "skipped" },
    { id: "f", label: "Send invitations", status: "canceled" },
  ];

  /** @type {TaskStep[]} */
  const details = [
    { id: "vpc", label: "Create VPC", status: "succeeded", startedAt: 0, endedAt: 12000, href: "#" },
    {
      id: "network",
      label: "Configure network",
      status: "failed",
      startedAt: 12000,
      endedAt: 43000,
      detail: "The gateway did not answer in time.",
      href: "#",
      error: "NetworkTimeout: gateway 10.240.0.1 did not answer within 30s\n  region: us-south-2\n  request: PUT /v1/gateways/gw-4f2a",
    },
    { id: "cluster", label: "Create cluster", status: "pending" },
  ];

  let expandedIds = ["network"];

  function clock(ms) {
    const seconds = Math.floor(ms / 1000);
    return `${Math.floor(seconds / 60)}:${String(seconds % 60).padStart(2, "0")}`;
  }
</script>

## Basic

Set `steps` to the steps in order. Each has an `id`, a `label` and a `status`: `"pending"`, `"running"`, `"succeeded"`, `"failed"`, `"skipped"` or `"canceled"`. The list shows the steps; it does not run them. Replace the array, or a step in it, as the work moves on. A summary line above the steps adds them up.

Name the list with `aria-label`.

<TaskStatusList aria-label="Cluster provisioning" {steps} />

## Statuses

Each status has its own icon shape and its own text after the step's label, which screen readers read, so a status never depends on color. A step that runs is the current step. Skipped steps were not needed, and canceled steps did not run or were stopped.

<TaskStatusList aria-label="Statuses" steps={statuses} />

## Time

A step with `startedAt` and `endedAt`, in milliseconds or as a `Date`, shows how long it took. A running step with `startedAt` shows how long it has run, counting up each second. One timer serves the whole list and runs only while a step does. The server and the first render show no running time, so hydration matches.

Set `durationText` to write the time another way, such as m:ss. It receives the length in milliseconds.

<TaskStatusList aria-label="Cluster provisioning, as a clock" {steps} durationText={clock} />

## Details and errors

A step can have a `detail` line under its label, an `href` to its log, and an `error`: the failure in full, as a string or an `Error`. The error opens under the step's Show details button, which names the step it belongs to. Bind `expandedIds` to open errors from your own code, such as to open the first failure.

<TaskStatusList aria-label="Network setup" steps={details} bind:expandedIds />

## Sizes

Set `size` to `"sm"` for a compact list in a modal or a side panel. The default is `"md"`. Set `hideSummary` when a heading near the list already says how far along it is.

<TaskStatusList aria-label="Cluster provisioning, compact" size="sm" {steps} />

## Running the steps

### With createTaskRunner

`createTaskRunner` runs the tasks and keeps the steps. It is a store: pass `$runner.steps` to the list. Each task is `{ id, label, run }`. `run` receives an `AbortSignal` that `cancel()` aborts, and `setDetail` to replace the step's detail line while it works.

Set `cancelable` and `retryable` to show Cancel, Retry from the failed step and Retry all. The buttons dispatch `cancel` and `retry`; call the runner from them. A failure stops the run at that step. Retry runs the failed step and the steps after it again, and keeps what finished. The Configure network step fails the first time.

<FileSource src="/framed/TaskStatusList/ProvisionCluster" />

```svelte
const runner = createTaskRunner([
  { id: "vpc", label: "Create VPC", run: createVpc },
  { id: "cluster", label: "Create cluster", run: createCluster },
]);
```

### Bulk operations

Set `concurrency` to run several tasks at once, and `onError` to `"continue"` to run the rest after a failure. The list then reads "2 steps failed" and offers Retry failed steps, which runs only the failed steps again.

<FileSource src="/framed/TaskStatusList/BulkDelete" />

### Your own steps

The runner is optional. Anything that can describe its steps can fill `steps`: an upload queue, a server job you poll, or a wizard that already tracks its progress. Set the status, and `startedAt` and `endedAt` as they change.

```svelte
<TaskStatusList
  aria-label="Import"
  steps={job.steps}
  retryable
  on:retry={() => api.retry(job.id)}
/>
```

## In a modal

Set `size="sm"` and put the actions in the modal's footer, where users look for them. Leave `cancelable` and `retryable` off and call the runner from the footer buttons. Closing the modal cancels the run.

<FileSource src="/framed/TaskStatusList/ModalProgress" />

## Announcements

Screen readers hear changes, not content. Nothing is read as the list mounts or as a time counts up.

| Change | Announced |
| --- | --- |
| The steps finish | "All 5 steps complete", at once |
| A step fails | "Failed at step 4: Configure network", at once and assertive |
| The run is canceled | "Canceled, 2 of 5 complete", at once |
| Steps start and finish | "2 of 5 complete: Create cluster", at most once per `announceDelay` (1 second), only the newest |

Fifty steps that finish in a second say the first message and the last one. Set `announceText` to change the words, or `announce` to `false` to say nothing. Focus does not move on a failure; the announcement and the Retry button tell the user what happened. When the button a user pressed leaves, such as Cancel after a cancel, focus moves to the next action, or to the summary.

Where the user prefers reduced motion, a running step shows a static half-filled circle instead of the spinner. In forced colors, every icon reads in the text color and keeps its shape.

## Usage

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Name the list with `aria-label`, such as "Cluster provisioning". | A screen reader says what the steps belong to when it reaches the list. |
| Give every step a unique `id`. | The `id` keys the row, so a status change updates one row, and it keeps the open error with its step. |
| Keep labels to what the step does, such as "Create subnets". | The label is read before the status, so the pair reads as one sentence. |
| Put long output in `error` and a short reason in `detail`. | The detail line stays visible at a glance; the error opens when a user wants it. |
| Show times only for steps that take seconds or more. | A step under a second reads as less than one second, which tells the user nothing. |
| Use ProgressIndicator for steps the user moves through. | A task status list reports work that runs by itself. |
| Use ProgressBar for one percentage, and InlineLoading for one action. | The list is for a handful of named steps, each with its own outcome. |
